//! crypto/ed25519_sign.rye -- Season G (Cryptography), the thirteenth rung: Ed25519 SIGNING authored in pure Rye, the near side of the Lotus signed
//! carry. Verification (the eleventh rung) reads only public values; signing is its mirror -- given a 32-byte secret seed and a message, it produces
//! the 64-byte signature R ‖ S that verify accepts. With the scalar multiply-add GREEN (crypto/ed25519_muladd.rye, the twelfth rung), every
//! arithmetic piece signing needs now stands, and this rung writes NO new mathematics: it composes the GREEN SHA-512, the GREEN scalar reduction
//! mod L, the GREEN scalar ladder over the derived base point, the GREEN point compression, and the GREEN multiply-add into the exact procedure
//! RFC 8032 §5.1.6 defines. A Rye-native Ed25519 keypair-and-signature now stands whole beside the verifier -- the signing side of Kumara identity,
//! Vault, Comlink, and the Lotus "who made this record" carry, all with no std.crypto and no C library.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names Ed25519 as the Lotus unblock;
//! "once a Rye signing primitive is parity-GREEN, Lotus's signed carry becomes buildable -- the library is engineering").
//! Kin: crypto/ed25519_muladd.rye (S = (r + k-a) mod L, GREEN) - crypto/ed25519_scalar.rye (reduce, GREEN) - crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye (the
//! ladder, GREEN) - crypto/ed25519_group.rye (point compression, GREEN) - crypto/ed25519_decode.rye (base-point derivation, GREEN) -
//! crypto/sha512.rye (GREEN) - crypto/ed25519_verify.rye (the mirror this rung round-trips through, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher,
//! CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line -- our own composition, studied clean-room from RFC 8032 (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX -- the RFC 8032 §5.1.6 signing procedure, composed. Expand the seed once through SHA-512: the low 32 bytes CLAMP into the secret scalar
//! a (clear the low three bits, clear the top bit, set the second-top -- the standard cofactor-and-range clamp), the high 32 bytes are the prefix.
//! The public key is A = compress([a]B). To sign a message M: derive the per-message nonce r = SHA-512(prefix ‖ M) mod L, form the commitment
//! R = compress([r]B), derive the challenge k = SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) mod L, and set S = (r + k-a) mod L over the GREEN multiply-add. The signature is
//! R ‖ S. The base point B is DERIVED (decode of the canonical encoding of y = 4/5, even x), never a pasted literal -- the same derivation the
//! verifier uses. Signing is deterministic: the same seed and message always yield the same signature.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device. This rung DOES hold a secret scalar and DOES sign -- but only
//! over TEST seeds a selftest generates or the caller supplies; a test seed is not the maintainer's identity key. Signing with the maintainer's own
//! identity key stays its own separate, GATED round (gate %3/%4): this file never reads a real key, never touches the network, and the design's own
//! word is that "the library is engineering" while "signing with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate." The secret scalar makes
//! the whole path timing-sensitive: this rung is authored for correctness and PARITY first -- a constant-time signing path (constant-time ladder,
//! reduction, and multiply-add over secret data) is the Season's named timing-safety HORIZON, wanting measurement rather than a claim, exactly the
//! posture the ladder, reduce, and multiply-add each already carry. Parity is proven at selftest against Zig's own std.crypto.sign.Ed25519 as an
//! independent RFC 8032 implementation -- across a spread of deterministic seeds and messages, our derived public key equals Zig's byte-for-byte and
//! our signature equals Zig's byte-for-byte -- and by round-tripping every signature back through our own GREEN verifier (genuine accepts, a one-byte
//! tamper is refused), and by confirming signing is deterministic. Monocypher-source parity stays the same held-fetch horizon the other primitives
//! carry.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/ed25519_sign.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const fe = @import("fe25519.rye");
const pt = @import("ed25519_group.rye");
const dec = @import("ed25519_decode.rye");
const sm = @import("ed25519_scalarmul.rye");
const sc = @import("ed25519_scalar.rye");
const ma = @import("ed25519_muladd.rye");
const sha512 = @import("sha512.rye");
const ver = @import("ed25519_verify.rye");

pub const ed25519_sign_version = "20260816.104452";

/// The largest message this signer hashes in one call. Signing hashes prefix ‖ M for the nonce and R ‖ A ‖ M for the challenge, each held in one
/// bounded buffer, so the message is capped at a named maximum rather than an open size -- the same bound the verifier carries. A streaming hash for
/// larger payloads is a separate, later rung; 64 KiB covers every signed record this stack produces near-term.
pub const max_sign_message_bytes: u32 = 1 << 16;

/// The ways signing can refuse. A message longer than the named bound is refused before any hashing -- the only edge failure a total signing
/// procedure has, since every scalar and point operation below is defined for all inputs.
pub const SignError = error{
    /// The message exceeds max_sign_message_bytes -- refused before hashing.
    MessageTooLong,
};

/// The edwards25519 base point B, DERIVED from its defining form: B has y = 4/5 and the even (sign-bit-clear) x, so decoding the canonical encoding
/// of y = 4/5 recovers exactly B in our own arithmetic. Identical to the verifier's derivation -- no borrowed base-point literal enters the tree.
fn base_point() pt.Point {
    const four = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 4, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
    const five = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 5, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
    const y = fe.mul(four, fe.invert(five)); // 4/5 mod p
    var enc = fe.to_bytes(y); // y < p < 2^255, so the top (sign) bit is already clear — the even-x branch
    // invariant: the encoding's sign bit is clear, so decode recovers the even-x base point, not its negation.
    assert((enc[31] & 0x80) == 0);
    const b = dec.decode(&enc) catch unreachable;
    // invariant: the recovered point is affine (Z = 1), the shape the ladder and group law expect.
    assert(b.z.l[0] == 1);
    return b;
}

/// The secret material a seed expands into: the clamped secret scalar a (the low SHA-512 half) and the prefix (the high half) that seeds the nonce.
pub const Expanded = struct {
    a: [32]u8,
    prefix: [32]u8,
};

/// Expand a 32-byte seed into (clamped secret scalar, prefix) by one SHA-512, exactly as RFC 8032 §5.1.5 specifies. The clamp clears the low three
/// bits (a multiple of the cofactor 8), clears the top bit and sets the second-top (range), so [a]B lands in the prime-order subgroup. Holds secret
/// material -- the timing-safety horizon covers this path.
fn expand(seed: *const [32]u8) Expanded {
    // SHA-512 of a 32-byte seed is far within sha512's own bound, so the hash cannot refuse here.
    const h = sha512.hash(seed) catch unreachable;
    var e: Expanded = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) {
        e.a[i] = h[i];
        e.prefix[i] = h[32 + i];
    }
    // The standard Ed25519 clamp: clear the low three bits, clear bit 255, set bit 254.
    e.a[0] &= 248;
    e.a[31] &= 127;
    e.a[31] |= 64;
    // invariant: the clamp cleared the low three bits, so a is a multiple of the cofactor 8.
    assert((e.a[0] & 7) == 0);
    // invariant: the clamp fixed the top two bits -- bit 255 clear, bit 254 set -- so a lies in the intended range.
    assert((e.a[31] & 0xc0) == 0x40);
    return e;
}

/// Derive the 32-byte Ed25519 public key A = compress([a]B) from a 32-byte secret seed. Reads a secret seed; produces only the public key, which is
/// safe to publish. The signing timing horizon covers the secret ladder here.
pub fn derive_public(seed: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    const e = expand(seed);
    const a_point = sm.scalarmul(base_point(), &e.a);
    const a_bytes = pt.to_bytes(a_point);
    // invariant: derivation is a pure function of the seed -- re-deriving yields the identical key.
    assert(bytes_eq(&a_bytes, &pt.to_bytes(sm.scalarmul(base_point(), &e.a))));
    // invariant: a clamped nonzero scalar times the prime-order base point is never the identity, so the key names a real point (identity
    // compresses to y = 1, the encoding 01 00...00).
    var ident = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
    ident[0] = 1;
    // invariant: a public key equal to the identity point signs nothing anyone can verify, and
    // it is the one derived value this must never publish
    assert(!bytes_eq(&a_bytes, &ident));
    return a_bytes;
}

/// secret_scalar -- the 32-byte clamped Ed25519 secret scalar a = clamp(SHA-512(seed)[0..32]), the value RFC 8032 §5.1.5 folds a seed into before
/// [a]B. It is ALSO exactly the X25519 secret key libsodium's crypto_sign_ed25519_sk_to_curve25519 (and Zig's X25519.KeyPair.fromEd25519) derive
/// from an Ed25519 seed, so one identity key agrees as well as signs -- crypto/ed25519_to_x25519.rye names this the secret half of its map. Reads
/// secret material and returns it, so a caller holds the raw scalar: the signing timing horizon covers this path, and deriving from a real seed
/// reaches the custody gate.
pub fn secret_scalar(seed: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    const e = expand(seed);
    // invariant (postcondition): the clamp cleared the low three bits, so the scalar is a multiple of the cofactor 8.
    assert((e.a[0] & 7) == 0);
    // invariant (postcondition): the clamp fixed the top two bits -- bit 255 clear, bit 254 set -- so the scalar lies in the intended range.
    assert((e.a[31] & 0xc0) == 0x40);
    return e.a;
}

/// Sign a message under a 32-byte secret seed, producing the 64-byte Ed25519 signature R ‖ S by the RFC 8032 §5.1.6 procedure. Deterministic: the
/// same seed and message always yield the same signature. Holds a secret scalar -- the constant-time signing path is the Season's named horizon.
pub fn sign(seed: *const [32]u8, msg: []const u8) SignError![64]u8 {
    // invariant: the message fits the named bound, so each hash buffer holds its whole prefix-plus-message.
    if (msg.len > max_sign_message_bytes) return error.MessageTooLong;
    const mlen: u32 = @intCast(msg.len);
    // invariant: the bounded cast lost nothing -- mlen names exactly the message bytes.
    assert(mlen == msg.len);

    const e = expand(seed);
    const a_bytes = pt.to_bytes(sm.scalarmul(base_point(), &e.a)); // A = compress([a]B)

    // r = SHA-512(prefix ‖ M) mod L -- the per-message nonce, over one bounded buffer.
    var rin: [32 + max_sign_message_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) rin[i] = e.prefix[i];
    i = 0;
    while (i < mlen) : (i += 1) rin[32 + i] = msg[i];
    const rdig = sha512.hash(rin[0 .. 32 + mlen]) catch unreachable;
    const r = sc.reduce(&rdig);

    // R = compress([r]B) -- the commitment.
    const r_bytes = pt.to_bytes(sm.scalarmul(base_point(), &r));

    // k = SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) mod L -- the challenge, over one bounded buffer.
    var kin: [64 + max_sign_message_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    i = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) kin[i] = r_bytes[i];
    i = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) kin[32 + i] = a_bytes[i];
    i = 0;
    while (i < mlen) : (i += 1) kin[64 + i] = msg[i];
    const kdig = sha512.hash(kin[0 .. 64 + mlen]) catch unreachable;
    const k = sc.reduce(&kdig);

    // S = (r + k-a) mod L over the GREEN multiply-add.
    const s_bytes = ma.mul_add(&k, &e.a, &r);
    // invariant: S is the canonical scalar below L, exactly the form verify demands of a signature's high half.
    assert(sc.is_canonical(&s_bytes));

    var sig: [64]u8 = undefined;
    i = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) {
        sig[i] = r_bytes[i];
        sig[32 + i] = s_bytes[i];
    }
    return sig;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Independent reference: Zig's own std.crypto Ed25519, an independent RFC 8032 implementation, used ONLY inside the selftest to cross-check the
/// derived public key and the signature byte-for-byte. The primitive above never calls it.
const ZSig = std.crypto.sign.Ed25519;

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const messages = [_][]const u8{
        "",
        "a",
        "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog",
        "grain — a personal server for whoever runs the software",
        "0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef",
    };

    // 1. Independent-implementation parity: across a spread of deterministic seeds and messages, our derived public key equals Zig's byte-for-byte
    //    and our signature equals Zig's byte-for-byte -- Zig being an independent RFC 8032 implementation.
    var seed_n: u8 = 1;
    while (seed_n <= 8) : (seed_n += 1) {
        const seed = [_]u8{seed_n} ** 32;

        const kp = ZSig.KeyPair.generateDeterministic(seed) catch return error.KeyGenFailed;
        const zpk = kp.public_key.toBytes();
        const ours_pk = derive_public(&seed);
        if (!bytes_eq(&ours_pk, &zpk)) return error.PublicKeyMismatch;

        for (messages) |m| {
            const zsig = (kp.sign(m, null) catch return error.SignFailed).toBytes();
            const ours = sign(&seed, m) catch return error.OurSignFailed;

            // Byte-for-byte agreement with the independent implementation.
            if (!bytes_eq(&ours, &zsig)) return error.SignatureMismatch;

            // Round-trip through our own GREEN verifier: the genuine signature accepts under our derived key.
            ver.verify(&ours, m, &ours_pk) catch return error.OwnVerifyRejected;

            // A one-byte tamper of our signature is refused by our own verifier.
            var bad = ours;
            bad[20] ^= 0x08;
            if (ver.verify(&bad, m, &ours_pk)) |_| return error.TamperAccepted else |_| {}

            // Signing is deterministic: the same seed and message yield the identical bytes.
            const again = sign(&seed, m) catch return error.OurSignFailed;
            if (!bytes_eq(&ours, &again)) return error.NotDeterministic;
        }
    }

    // 2. The named message bound is enforced before hashing -- a message one byte past the maximum is refused with the named error.
    {
        const seed = [_]u8{9} ** 32;
        var big: [max_sign_message_bytes + 1]u8 = undefined;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < big.len) : (i += 1) big[i] = 0;
        if (sign(&seed, big[0..])) |_| return error.OversizeAccepted else |err| {
            if (err != error.MessageTooLong) return error.WrongOversizeError;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-ed25519-sign: Ed25519 signing authored in pure Rye — Season G's thirteenth rung, the near side of the Lotus signed carry. " ++
        "Given a 32-byte secret seed and a message, sign produces the 64-byte signature R ‖ S the verifier accepts, by the RFC 8032 §5.1.6 " ++
        "procedure: expand the seed through SHA-512 into the clamped secret scalar a and the prefix, publish A = compress([a]B), derive the nonce " ++
        "r = SHA-512(prefix ‖ M) mod L, form R = compress([r]B), derive the challenge k = SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) mod L, and set S = (r + k·a) mod L " ++
        "over the GREEN multiply-add. It writes no new mathematics — it composes the GREEN SHA-512, scalar reduction mod L, scalar ladder, point " ++
        "compression, and multiply-add. The base point B is DERIVED (decode of the canonical encoding of y = 4/5), never a pasted literal, the " ++
        "same derivation the verifier uses. Signing is deterministic. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity holds against Zig's independent " ++
        "std.crypto.sign.Ed25519 — across a spread of deterministic seeds and messages, our derived public key equals Zig's byte-for-byte and our " ++
        "signature equals Zig's byte-for-byte — every signature round-trips through our own GREEN verifier (genuine accepts, a one-byte tamper is " ++
        "refused), signing is confirmed deterministic, and a message past the named bound is refused. Monocypher's own signing passes the same " ++
        "mathematics — its vendored source is a held fetch, so Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. This rung holds a secret scalar " ++
        "and signs, so a constant-time signing path is the Season's named timing HORIZON, wanting measurement not a claim. Purely local — it signs " ++
        "only TEST seeds, never the network, never funds, never a real device; signing with the maintainer's own identity key stays its own " ++
        "separate, GATED round. A Rye-native Ed25519 keypair-and-signature now stands whole beside the verifier — Kumara identity, Vault, Comlink, " ++
        "and the Lotus signed carry can sign and verify with no std.crypto or C library, the maintainer's own key awaiting only its gate.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
